WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.610
Could it be that the most dangerous region
in the world is trying to improve itself?

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:08.970
Imagine that you’re sitting quietly at a
restaurant or cafeteria and, suddenly, you

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:11.610
begin to hear gunshots a few meters away.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:15.230
You’d choose an empty stomach and opt for
a quick escape, wouldn’t you?

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:20.990
Well, unfortunately these kinds of situations
are common in many Central American cities.

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:25.400
Cities where security is noticeable for its
absence.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:34.770
In 2018, 42 of the 50 most violent cities
in the world were in Latin America, and of

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:38.550
these, two were in Honduras, one in Guatemala
and one in El Salvador.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:44.760
In 2017 alone, more than 14,500 homicides
were registered in Central America.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:50.430
The vast majority, more than 90% of them,
happened in three countries known as the Northern

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:55.710
Triangle: El Salvador, Honduras and Guatemala.

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:58.910
Chilling, to say the least.

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:01.649
So why is violence so common in this part
of the world?

00:01:01.649 --> 00:01:03.790
What is driving these high crime rates?

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:06.690
Has Central America always been a violent
region?

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:09.690
Let’s find out.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:12.140
(INTRO)

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:20.790
Here’s a question for you: Have you ever
noticed how curious the map of the American

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:21.890
continent is?

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:26.750
Just look at it for a second, Central America
is a bridge between South America and North

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:27.750
America.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:28.750
And not only metaphorically, but also literally.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.820
And this has consequences:

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:36.110
The fact that Central America is a bridge
has turned it into the largest illicit goods

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:37.500
highway in the world.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.450
For goods produced in South America.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:46.780
We’re mostly talking about cocaine.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:51.770
No less than 90% of all the cocaine that enters
the United States, the world's largest market

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:58.670
for the drug, passes through Central America.

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:02.280
But don’t think this is the only illegal
activity based there.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:03.280
Not at all.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:07.130
Human trafficking; trafficking of illegal
immigrants and minors, is also booming in

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:08.130
this region.

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:12.120
And as you can imagine, all of these activities
go hand in hand with organized crime; criminal

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:20.150
organizations that control, manage and fight
each other for dominance of all these businesses.

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:27.940
We’re talking about a huge industry, and
a highly structured one: with managers, administrators,

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:32.840
lawyers, corporate advisors, diversification,
investment strategies, etc.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:37.540
An industry that is increasingly becoming
involved in developing a presence in Central

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:38.540
America.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:39.540
Check it out.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:43.510
(AUDIO: Mexican cartels are moving 90% of
their activities to Central America, according

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:44.510
to research.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:45.510
- Agencia EFE)

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:48.220
The increasing pressure from the state in
Mexico in recent years has made this type

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:51.900
of mafia want to settle in countries with
weaker and less vigilant governments.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:52.940
And you know what?

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:58.120
The truth is that these groups, these macro-crime
companies have managed to take over much of

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:01.100
Central America.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:06.260
(MEXICO, CRIME EXPORTER TO CENTRAL AMERICA)

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:12.599
Mexico – or rather the Mexican cartels – are
slowly becoming the leaders, the biggest drug

00:03:12.599 --> 00:03:14.700
business owners in Latin America.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:22.900
Of course, they’ve been competing with Colombian
groups for quite some time.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:27.670
The Mexican cartels already control the majority
of the main routes for transporting illegal

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:28.670
goods.

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:33.750
And that pretty much means controlling all
of Central America.

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:39.569
Now, let's put ourselves in the shoes of these
great bosses for a second.

00:03:39.569 --> 00:03:40.970
Imagine that you’re one of them.

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:44.620
What’s the fastest way to control the Central
American territory?

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:49.660
How can you settle in and dominate this region?

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:57.920
Exactly, a good option would be to establish
alliances with the native criminal groups,

00:03:57.920 --> 00:03:59.459
those that were there before you.

00:03:59.459 --> 00:04:04.030
Groups that have the necessary infrastructure,
contacts and knowledge of the territory that

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:05.030
you need.

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:09.370
We’re talking about groups that have a single
philosophy: work with whoever gives you the

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:10.370
most cash.

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:11.890
This is hardly surprising.

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:17.549
We all know the power of the dollar.

00:04:17.549 --> 00:04:22.639
But how and why did Central America become
so ripe for exploitation by the Mexican drug

00:04:22.639 --> 00:04:23.639
traffickers?

00:04:23.639 --> 00:04:26.830
To understand that we need to go back to the
90s.

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:34.369
You see, the key moment, when the players
changed, happened between 1991 and 1993, when

00:04:34.369 --> 00:04:40.389
the Medellín Cartel suffered its greatest
defeat.

00:04:40.389 --> 00:04:45.099
Pablo Escobar’s fall, as well as that of
the cartel’s main bosses, also meant losing

00:04:45.099 --> 00:04:49.529
the huge influence and power they had in Central
America, which they’d gained thanks to their

00:04:49.529 --> 00:04:56.879
alliances with local groups, who worked directly
for them.

00:04:56.879 --> 00:05:01.229
And that was the Mexican groups’ great window
of opportunity, which of course they took

00:05:01.229 --> 00:05:06.119
advantage of by storming into Central America
with the objective of completely controlling

00:05:06.119 --> 00:05:14.969
that important bridge, which connects the
production centers with the consumption centers.

00:05:14.969 --> 00:05:16.460
But surely some of you may be thinking.

00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:21.069
Just a second, Simon, there are several different
cartels in Mexico, didn’t that pose a problem?

00:05:21.069 --> 00:05:26.759
Yes, indeed, it did.

00:05:26.759 --> 00:05:30.939
After the Mexicans arrived, Central America
became a kind of battlefield between the different

00:05:30.939 --> 00:05:34.240
Mexican cartels who fought to control the
transport routes.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:37.930
And that’s precisely one of the reasons
that violence in Central America increased

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:42.149
dramatically until finally each cartel claimed
their own niche territory.

00:05:42.149 --> 00:05:46.699
You could say that Mexican cartels became
major exporters of violence and crime to their

00:05:46.699 --> 00:05:47.759
southern neighbors.

00:05:47.759 --> 00:05:51.839
Check out the case of Honduras for example.

00:05:51.839 --> 00:05:53.710
(CHART)

00:05:53.710 --> 00:06:01.509
You see, the power struggles between Mexican
cartels and local groups to increase their

00:06:01.509 --> 00:06:07.159
influence and gain control of the business
explain much of the increased crime during

00:06:07.159 --> 00:06:08.159
those years.

00:06:08.159 --> 00:06:10.089
Now, let's put all big numbers aside.

00:06:10.089 --> 00:06:11.620
Let's focus on the streets.

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:16.580
How exactly do all these battles between cartels
and Central American criminal groups affect

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:18.550
the safety of regular citizens?

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:20.539
Listen up.

00:06:20.539 --> 00:06:26.990
(WHEN VIOLENCE DOESN’T FIND AN ENEMY)

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:31.819
We’ve already showed you that the Central
American North Triangle has the highest crime

00:06:31.819 --> 00:06:33.150
rates in the world.

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:36.369
That’s why we ask an essential question,
is there a solution?

00:06:36.369 --> 00:06:40.759
What does the region need to achieve social
peace?

00:06:40.759 --> 00:06:46.319
Well, the truth is that this goal seems very,
very complicated, why?

00:06:46.319 --> 00:06:47.319
Check this out…

00:06:47.319 --> 00:06:50.430
(AUDIO: Honduran President’s Brother Is
Found Guilty of Drug Trafficking.

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:51.469
- The New York Times)

00:06:51.469 --> 00:06:53.869
(AUDIO: Honduran President Is Accused of Drug
Conspiracy.

00:06:53.869 --> 00:06:59.099
Prosecutors say drug proceeds were allegedly
used to support Honduran President Juan Orlando

00:06:59.099 --> 00:07:01.610
Hernández’s 2013 election WSJ)

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:05.990
(AUDIO: Son of ex-Honduran president Porfirio
Lobo gets 24 years for U.S. drug charge.

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:06.990
Reuters)

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:11.159
(AUDIO: Former El Salvador President Saca
gets two-year prison term for bribery.

00:07:11.159 --> 00:07:12.499
- Reuters)

00:07:12.499 --> 00:07:18.220
Against such a backdrop of corruption, institutionalized
drug-trafficking and other despicable actions,

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:20.969
who would put up their hand to fight crime?

00:07:20.969 --> 00:07:22.849
Especially if crime itself is in charge!

00:07:22.849 --> 00:07:27.649
If the highest spheres of power in Central
America have such close ties, very close,

00:07:27.649 --> 00:07:35.440
with the great criminals, how on earth is
anyone going to fight them?

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.729
And it’s not that they can’t, but that
they don’t want to.

00:07:37.729 --> 00:07:38.929
It’s a matter of incentives.

00:07:38.929 --> 00:07:43.419
No one, absolutely no one wants to kill the
goose that lays the golden eggs.

00:07:43.419 --> 00:07:44.899
However, just a second.

00:07:44.899 --> 00:07:48.789
Because despite this harsh reality, the truth
is that in recent years, Central America has

00:07:48.789 --> 00:07:52.050
experienced a gradual reduction in homicide
rates.

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:54.149
This has led many politicians to stand up.

00:07:54.149 --> 00:07:58.679
Of course, in many cases, it wasn’t thanks
to the government, but in spite of it.

00:07:58.679 --> 00:08:00.839
Let's take a look at El Salvador.

00:08:00.839 --> 00:08:09.199
For example, in El Salvador in 2018, they
had 15% fewer homicides than in the previous

00:08:09.199 --> 00:08:15.520
year, but even so, it was still a very high
number: 3,340 homicides in one year in a country

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:23.110
of less than six and a half million inhabitants.

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:27.169
El Salvador is the country with the highest
homicide rate in the most dangerous region

00:08:27.169 --> 00:08:28.169
on the planet.

00:08:28.169 --> 00:08:35.289
So we could say it’s the most dangerous
country in the world.

00:08:35.289 --> 00:08:39.110
But even though the country has encouraged
it, the truth is that this history of violence

00:08:39.110 --> 00:08:46.350
comes from way back, long before the Mexican
cartels ever set foot in Central America.

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:50.670
We’ve talked about them for a while on VisualPolitik.

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:55.300
The Maras are dangerous criminal gang member
groups, many based in Los Angeles, that are

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:59.790
dedicated mainly to drug and weapons trafficking,
money laundering, immigrant trafficking and

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:02.329
prostitution.

00:09:02.329 --> 00:09:08.740
Since the 1960s, they’ve been one of El
Salvador’s greatest security challenges.

00:09:08.740 --> 00:09:15.300
The Mara Salvatrucha or MS13 and Barrio 18
are the two main branches which, as expected,

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:19.220
often fight each other, and have been trying
to control the Salvadoran territory for many

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:23.290
years in order to better carry out their illegal
activities.

00:09:23.290 --> 00:09:30.339
It has been this fight over the territory
that has spread pain and suffering all over

00:09:30.339 --> 00:09:33.029
El Salvador.

00:09:33.029 --> 00:09:37.839
And of course, these maras are still fully
active.

00:09:37.839 --> 00:09:40.930
So why is it that crime has decreased in recent
years?

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:42.080
Well... take a look at this...

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:46.000
(AUDIO: Drug traffickers use maras and street
gangs to dominate Central America.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:47.000
- Excelsior)

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:51.170
Yes, as you can see, paradoxically this alliance
could explain much of the reduction in homicides.

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:52.170
Why?

00:09:52.170 --> 00:09:53.170
Well...

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:56.480
The answer is complex, but simplifying it
a bit, we could say that currently the maras

00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:02.620
are acting as branches of the most powerful
Mexican cartels, such as the Sinaloa Cartel

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:07.470
or Los Zetas.

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:13.040
Interestingly, this alliance could be causing
part of these gangs’ forces to be operating

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:17.650
in wider fields, protecting the Mexican cartels’
activities, and thus moving them away from

00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:18.650
the streets.

00:10:18.650 --> 00:10:22.600
Let's say there are bigger businesses than
extorting money from the corner store.

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:24.459
And that’s not the only change.

00:10:24.459 --> 00:10:28.400
In addition, Mexican cartels have noticed
that the Maras are tremendously effective

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:32.110
in controlling cities and infusing the greatest
possible terror.

00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:35.899
So, they are recruiting troops and moving
to Mexico to support their activities and

00:10:35.899 --> 00:10:37.110
to train their own people.

00:10:37.110 --> 00:10:43.230
Basically they are enlisting them.

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:48.290
That way, Mexicans have introduced the maras
into the large-scale international drug trafficking

00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:49.290
business.

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:51.880
But how exactly are the gangs helping the
Mexican cartels?

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:58.399
Well, obviously not for the common good.

00:10:58.399 --> 00:11:02.509
They are teaching them the most horrible,
cruel and ruthless ways of treating their

00:11:02.509 --> 00:11:03.509
adversaries.

00:11:03.509 --> 00:11:08.360
These are techniques they are moving to and
implementing in Mexico itself.

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:15.410
I think that now you can understand better
why crime has decreased in recent years.

00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:19.580
And yes, unfortunately, it has had little
to do with governments or with the US’s

00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:28.170
plan for Central America, and more with the
dynamics of the crime industry.

00:11:28.170 --> 00:11:33.600
Surely at this point many may be thinking
that this is getting out of hand, right?

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:38.190
Maras and cartels collaborating, governments
bought by and backing criminal interests,

00:11:38.190 --> 00:11:41.620
ineffective and corrupt security forces, bribed
courts...

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:45.420
Against such a backdrop, do you think that
El Salvador and Central America in general

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:47.810
have any chance to escape this vicious circle?

00:11:47.810 --> 00:11:51.820
What can Salvadorans do to stop being the
most dangerous country in the world?

00:11:51.820 --> 00:11:55.490
Well, I'm sorry to say that it doesn't look
like an easy task.

00:11:55.490 --> 00:12:02.259
But who knows, perhaps the arrival of this
man, who’s very well-known for leading his

00:12:02.259 --> 00:12:06.640
government through tweets, can make a step
forward by showing that one of the problems

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:11.370
of Central America, and perhaps the most important
one, is that there’s a lack of political

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:14.130
will.

00:12:14.130 --> 00:12:25.000
(AUDIO: Nayib Bukele, an Outsider Candidate,
Claims Victory in El Salvador Election.

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:26.000
- The New York Times)

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:27.000
[TEXT: And here, on June 1st, we’ll build
the best government in our country’s history.]

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:30.810
Bukele came to power early in 2019 with the
key promise of making El Salvador a safer

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:31.959
and less corrupt country.

00:12:31.959 --> 00:12:33.210
Will he achieve it?

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:37.740
Only time will tell, but for now, it looks
like he’s taking decisive steps to do so.

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:39.959
Of course, with Latin American politicians...

00:12:39.959 --> 00:12:41.319
Well, no one ever knows.

00:12:41.319 --> 00:12:45.879
In this region the forecasts, especially if
they’re optimistic, aren’t worth very

00:12:45.879 --> 00:12:46.879
much.

00:12:46.879 --> 00:12:50.360
(AUDIO: El Salvador launches anti-corruption
commission, inspired by Guatemala.

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:51.360
- Reuters)

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:53.480
And be careful because corruption isn’t
a minor problem.

00:12:53.480 --> 00:13:00.769
In this region, fighting corruption is a necessary
step to break the link between power and crime.

00:13:00.769 --> 00:13:06.020
Well, having reached this point, we’ve seen
why politicians can’t take the credit, even

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:09.660
if homicide rates are decreasing; we’ve
seen that nowadays there’s little room for

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:16.970
hope in Honduras and that El Salvador could
be facing a great opportunity for real change.

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:24.240
However, in this delicate balance of crime
and power we need to include another Central

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:25.240
American country.

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:29.839
A country that isn’t part of the Northern
Triangle, but which has recently seen a dramatic

00:13:29.839 --> 00:13:31.139
increase in violence.

00:13:31.139 --> 00:13:35.060
In many cases, a very different violence,
because drug-trafficking isn’t the only

00:13:35.060 --> 00:13:37.220
disease that plagues Central America.

00:13:37.220 --> 00:13:38.220
Listen up.

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:42.720
[TEXT: I want them to leave the country.

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:44.009
I want them to go to jail.]

00:13:44.009 --> 00:13:46.220
(NICARAGUA, THE END OF A DREAM?)

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:49.550
We’ve talked about this in several videos.

00:13:49.550 --> 00:13:54.070
In Nicaragua there’s a phenomenon that doesn’t
happen in many other Latin American countries,

00:13:54.070 --> 00:13:59.370
that is the use of violence as a form of political
control.

00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:05.520
It’s a scenario similar to the one found
in Venezuela, and which has suddenly ended

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:10.550
the myth that Nicaragua was the exception;
the only safe country in the most dangerous

00:14:10.550 --> 00:14:12.699
region in the world.

00:14:12.699 --> 00:14:22.180
For example, in 2017 Nicaragua only had seven
homicides per 100,000 inhabitants, while at

00:14:22.180 --> 00:14:27.949
the same time in Honduras it was 40, it was
51 in El Salvador and 28 in Guatemala.

00:14:27.949 --> 00:14:32.949
The difference is remarkable, but that doesn’t
really mean Nicaragua is a safe place to live.

00:14:32.949 --> 00:14:36.339
Time has shown us that.

00:14:36.339 --> 00:14:42.649
The Sandinista National Liberation Front,
the ruling party, has completely penetrated

00:14:42.649 --> 00:14:47.520
the state power agencies, politicizing them
by getting like-minded people into key positions

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:55.660
and using true paramilitary groups to repress
the population.

00:14:55.660 --> 00:15:00.410
Nicaraguan tyrant Daniel Ortega has literally
begun employing thugs to do the dirtiest work

00:15:00.410 --> 00:15:10.009
in the name of the police and the army during
the protests.

00:15:10.009 --> 00:15:14.980
And yes, from a political and institutional
point of view, this is one of the worst decisions

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:15.980
a leader can make.

00:15:15.980 --> 00:15:22.069
(AUDIO: Alarm rises for a wave of crimes with
viciousness that bear the seal of the paramilitaries

00:15:22.069 --> 00:15:24.050
of Daniel Ortega in Nicaragua.

00:15:24.050 --> 00:15:25.050
- Infobae)

00:15:25.050 --> 00:15:29.550
(AUDIO: In 2018, violence rates skyrocketed
through the brutal repression of civil protesters

00:15:29.550 --> 00:15:31.529
by President Daniel Ortega's regime.

00:15:31.529 --> 00:15:32.529
InsightCrime)

00:15:32.529 --> 00:15:36.129
Because as soon as a government begins using
irregular force to repress the masses, they

00:15:36.129 --> 00:15:41.420
create conditions that legalize and normalize
violence and give impunity at a general level.

00:15:41.420 --> 00:15:46.699
You could say the floodgates of crime are
opened with the first blow.

00:15:46.699 --> 00:15:52.309
We’ve seen it in Venezuela and we’re starting
to see it in Nicaragua.

00:15:52.309 --> 00:15:56.480
Anyway, as you can see, crime, corruption
and a lack of safety are the great evils that

00:15:56.480 --> 00:15:58.339
are hanging over Central America.

00:15:58.339 --> 00:16:04.160
In many cases, if not in all, this is happening
with the approval and complicity of the political

